<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/16th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Treaty · Peace</p><h1 class="enc-title">Peace of Cambrai</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1529">1529</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a> · Also known as Ladies' Peace, Treaty of Cambrai (1529) · Part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/war-of-the-league-of-cognac">War of the League of Cognac</a> · <a href="/encyclopedia/wars">All wars</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Peace of Cambrai, called the Ladies' Peace, was signed on 3 August 1529 between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/francis-i-of-france">Francis I of France</a> and Emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-v-holy-roman-emperor">Charles V</a>, negotiated by Louise of Savoy and Margaret of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a>. Francis gave up his claims in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> and his suzerainty over <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/flanders">Flanders</a> and Artois and paid a ransom for his sons held in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>, while Charles dropped his claim to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burgundy">Burgundy</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/peace-of-cambrai.jpg" alt="Peace of Cambrai" width="320" height="438" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Bernard van Orley · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bernaerd_van_Orley_002.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>The peace ended French involvement in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/war-of-the-league-of-cognac">War of the League of Cognac</a>, which had lasted since 1526. It was negotiated and signed at Cambrai, the centre of a principality-bishopric in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-netherlands">Netherlands</a>, in what is now the French department of Nord. It is known as the Ladies' Peace because women prepared it: Louise of Savoy, mother of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/francis-i-of-france">Francis I</a>, represented the king, and Margaret of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a>, aunt of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-v-holy-roman-emperor">Charles V</a>, represented the emperor, helping both monarchs not to lose face. Marguerite of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-navarre">Navarre</a> was also instrumental. The treaty renewed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-madrid-1526">Treaty of Madrid</a> of 1526 and added clauses meant to settle long-standing disputes, mainly over the Burgundian inheritance.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Terms</h2><p>Francis renounced his claims to Italian lands, including the Kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/naples">Naples</a> and the Duchy of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/milan">Milan</a>. France accepted that the former Burgundian domains within the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/holy-roman-empire">Holy Roman Empire</a>, including the Free County of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burgundy">Burgundy</a>, would stay with the emperor, and gave up its suzerainty over the counties of Artois and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/flanders">Flanders</a>, which the emperor already held. Charles in turn accepted that the other Burgundian domains within the Kingdom of France, including the Duchy of Burgundy and the Picardian counties, would remain French.</p><p>The treaty provided for the return to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> of the Dauphin François and Prince Henry, the future Henry II, in exchange for a ransom of 2 million écus. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/francis-i-of-france">Francis I</a> confirmed his consent to marry <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-v-holy-roman-emperor">Charles V</a>'s sister Eleanor, and he married her on 7 July 1530.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>The treaty confirmed effective <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/habsburg-dynasty">Habsburg</a> hegemony in the Duchy of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/milan">Milan</a> and the Kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/naples">Naples</a>. Together with the Treaty of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/barcelona">Barcelona</a> between the emperor and the pope, concluded in June 1529, it brought about the disintegration of the Cognac League. Only the Florentine Republic went on fighting <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-v-holy-roman-emperor">Charles V</a>, which led to the siege and surrender of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/florence">Florence</a> in 1530. The terms did not satisfy <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/francis-i-duke-of-nevers">Francis I</a>, and in 1536 a new conflict began between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/holy-roman-empire">Holy Roman Empire</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Treaty of Cambrai” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The city hosted the League of Cambrai against Venice in 1508 and the <b>Ladies' Peace</b> of 1529.</blockquote><a href="/state/prince-bishopric-of-cambrai">Prince-Bishopric of Cambrai</a></li></ul></section>
